To draw a chessboard with the given FEN (Forsyth-Edwards Notation) state in LaTeX, follow these steps:

1. Include necessary packages and commands:
   Add the following lines at the beginning of your LaTeX document:
   ```
   \documentclass{article}
   \usepackage{tikz} % for drawing the board
   \usepackage{xstring} % for string manipulation
   \usetikzlibrary{positioning} % for relative node placement
   \newcommand{\squarewidth}{1.5cm} % set square width
   \newcommand{\rowsep}{\vspace{\squarewidth}} % define row separator
   ```
2. Parse the FEN notation to determine the board state:
   The FEN notation is a standardized format to represent the state of a chessboard. It comprises of six components:
   - Number of rows (from 8 down to 1)
   - White piece placement (starting from column A)
   - Empty squares (represented by '/') 
   - Black piece placement (from column H to A)
   - Castling availability (formatted as "KQkq" if all options available)
   - En passant target square (in lower case alphabetical format)
   For our purpose, we only need the piece placement information from the FEN notation.

   Parse the FEN using the following command:
   ```
   \StrSubstitute{<FEN>}{/}{}[\fenrow] % Extract row information
   \StrSubstitute{\fenrow}{ }{\&}[\fenrowb] % Replace spaces with ampersand for easier parsing
   \newcommand{\cols}{\StrCount{\fenrowb}{\&}} % Determine number of columns
   \newcommand{\row}{\StrBehind{\fenrow}{\&}{\cols}} % Get current row pieces as string
